om{\\rule{0.277778em}{0ex}}\\ge \\phantom{\\rule{0.277778em}{0ex}}\\alpha \\phantom{\\rule{0.166667em}{0ex}}{k}_{\\ell }\\phantom{\\rule{2.em}{0ex}}\\mathrm{for}\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\mathrm{all}\\phantom{\\rule{4.pt}{0ex}}\\ell .$$\n\nFrom the Block–Escape Property one can prove rigorously that the average block index diverges:\nIn other words, the system must satisfy\n\n$$\\left(\\mathrm{BEP}\\right)\\phantom{\\rule{1.em}{0ex}}⟹\\phantom{\\rule{1.em}{0ex}}\\underset{k\\to \\infty }{lim\\; sup}{\\displaystyle \\frac{J\\left(k\\right)}{k}}>0.$$\n\nWhy Conjecture 7.8 would complete the argument.\n\nIf Conjecture 7.8 holds, then for some $\\alpha >0$, along a subsequence ${k}_{\\ell }$ we have $J\\left({k}_{\\ell }\\right)\\ge \\alpha {k}_{\\ell }$. Since ${T}^{k}{n}_{0}\\in {I}_{J\\left(k\\right)}=[{6}^{J\\left(k\\right)},{6}^{J\\left(k\\right)+1})$, this implies\n\n$${T}^{{k}_{\\ell }}{n}_{0}\\phantom{\\rule{0.277778em}{0ex}}\\ge \\phantom{\\rule{0.277778em}{0ex}}{6}^{\\alpha {k}_{\\ell }}.$$\n\nCombined with the universal upper bound ${T}^{k}{n}_{0}\\le {2}^{k}{n}_{0}$, this yields\n\n$${6}^{\\alpha {k}_{\\ell }}\\le {2}^{{k}_{\\ell }}{n}_{0},$$\n\nwhich is impossible for any $\\alpha log6>log2$. Hence no infinite Collatz orbit can satisfy both BEP and the universal upper growth bound. Therefore Conjecture 7.8 would rule out all infinite orbits.\n\nThis precisely isolates the remaining forward-dynamical obstruction: establishing it would complete the block argument and, combined with the spectral theorem, imply the Collatz conjecture.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGreg Martin: Paper Abstract (https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/index.shtml?abstract=SSPCRT)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Conversely, we use a probabilistic construction of B^{*}[g] sets to establish an upper bound on Δ(ε) for small ε.Two of our other joint papers, Constructions of generalized Sidon sets and The supremum of autoconvolutions, with applications to additive number theory, are closely related.\n\n  * Download the paper The symmetric subset problem in continuous Ramsey theory in PDF format (official Experiment.\n\n#### The symmetric subset problem in continuous Ramsey theory\n\nwith Kevin O'Bryant A symmetric subset of the reals is one that remains invariant under some reflection z → c-z. Given 0 < ε ≤ 1, there exists a real number Δ(ε) with the following property: if 0 ≤ δ < Δ(ε), then every subset of [0,1] with measure ε contains a symmetric subset with measure δ, while if δ > Δ(ε), then there exists a subset of [0,1] with measure ε that does not contain a symmetric subset with measure δ. In this paper we establish upper and lower bounds for Δ(ε) of the same order of magnitude: for example, we prove that Δ(ε) = 2ε - 1 for 11/16 ≤ ε ≤ 1 and that 0.59ε^{2} < Δ(ε) < 0.8ε^{2} for 0 < ε ≤ 11/16.This continuous problem is intimately connected with a corresponding discrete problem. A set S of integers is called a B^{*}[g] set if for any given m, there are at most g ordered pairs (s_{1},s_{2}) ∈ S×S with s_{1} + s_{2} = m; in the case g = 2, these are better known as Sidon sets. Our lower bound on Δ(ε) implies that every B^{*}[g] set contained in {1, 2, ..., n} has cardinality less than 1.30036 √{gn}. This improves a result of Green for g ≥ 30. Conversely, we use a probabilistic construction of B^{*}[g] sets to establish an upper bound on Δ(ε) for small ε.Two of our other joint papers, Constructions of generalized Sidon sets and The supremum of autoconvolutions, with applications to additive number theory, are closely related.\n\n  * Download the paper The symmetric subset problem in continuous Ramsey theory in PDF format (official Experiment. Math. version)\n  * See the Math Review for this paper (restricted to MathSciNet subscribers): HTML or PDF format--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPROCEEDINGS OF THE (https://personal.m